- How would you test very large files near quota or timeout boundaries?
- How would you validate privacy across different browsers and network conditions?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests practical testing design for event-driven pipelines, focusing on end-to-end integrity, exactly-once semantics, dead-letter queues, and resilience under burst traffic—common challenges in fintech and ride-hailing domains.

## Key Concepts

- Event-driven architecture
- Exactly-once processing and idempotency
- Dead-letter queues and backpressure
- Failure injection and chaos testing
- Data integrity validation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test: ensure idempotent consumer
let lastProcessed = new Map();
async function process(event) {
  const key = event.id;
  if (lastProcessed.has(key)) {
    return { status: 'duplicate', result: lastProcessed.get(key) };
  }
  const result = await processEvent(event);
  lastProcessed.set(key, result);
  return { status: 'processed', result };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Real-time pipelines present subtle consistency and resilience challenges that unit tests alone cannot catch. Interviewers want to see that you understand end-to-end testing across distributed systems, including CI/CD integration.

## Key Concepts
- Exactly-once semantics via transactional sinks and checkpointing
- Stateful stream processing and its failure modes
- Backward/forward schema evolution to prevent consumer breakage
- Backpressure handling, failure recovery, and replay safety
- Observability within CI/CD pipelines

## Code Example
```javascript
// Verify exactly-once processing with idempotent writes
const result = await warehouse.query(`
  SELECT event_id, COUNT(*) as score_count
  FROM risk_scores
  GROUP BY event_id
  HAVING COUNT(*) > 1
`);
assert(result.length === 0, 'Duplicate events detected');

// Validate replay safety after simulated outage
await kafka.broker.crash({ node: 3 });
await flink.restartFromCheckpoint();
const postRecoveryScores = await warehouse.fetchLatestScores();
assert(postRecoveryScores.length === expectedCount);
```

